    echo "Job $id started\n";
    // Simulate job processing by sleeping
    Coroutine::sleep(rand(1, 3));
    echo "Job $id finished\n";
}

// Function to add jobs with coroutine concurrency control
function addJob($id, $channel) {
    // Wait for an available slot
    $channel->push(true);
    // Start the coroutine for the job
    Coroutine::create(function() use ($id, $channel) {
        job($id);
        // Signal that the job is finished
        $channel->pop();
    });
}

// Add jobs
for ($i = 1; $i <= 10; $i++) {
    addJob($i, $channel);
}

// Wait for all jobs to finish
for ($i = 1; $i <= MAX_CONCURRENT; $i++) {
    $channel->pop();
}
```

In this example, a `Channel` is used to control the concurrency of coroutines. The channel's capacity is set to the maximum number of concurrent coroutines allowed. When adding a job, it pushes a value into the channel, which will block if the channel is full, effectively limiting the number of running coroutines. After a coroutine finishes its job, it pops a value from the channel, allowing another coroutine to start.

